{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it shall come to pass, when all these things have come upon you, etc.</b> One must ponder: for at first it said, ״among all the nations where HaShem your Elohim has driven you — הִדִּיחֲךָ — there״, and afterward it said, ״among all the nations where HaShem your Elohim has scattered you — הֱפִיצְךָ — there״. For there is a distinction between the expression הֱפִיצְךָ ('scattered you') and the expression הִדִּיחֲךָ ('driven you'): for הֱפִיצְךָ is said regarding the exile, and הִדִּיחֲךָ is said regarding the commandments, for when Israel is in the lands of the nations they are thrust away from the commandments — both on account of what is said in the reproof (Deuteronomy 28:36), ״and you shall serve there other elohim,״ and on account of the fact that most of the commandments depend on the Land. And through this thrusting away Israel come close to falling into error and saying that the Holy One, blessed be He, no longer desires their deeds at all, in the manner of what is said (Isaiah 1:11), ״To what purpose is the multitude of your sacrifices to Me, says YHWH,״ and through this they will despair entirely of redemption, for they will say that the Holy One, blessed be He, thrust them away from the commandments with full intention. But this is an error on their part, for the Holy One, blessed be He, did not intend, essentially and in the first instance, anything but the scattering — to disperse them, this one away from that one, for the gathering of the wicked is bad for them (Sanhedrin 71b). However, the fact that they were thrust away from the commandments follows as a by-product of the scattering, and it is not that HaShem, blessed be He, intended, essentially and in the first instance, to thrust them away from the commandments." "And this is what is intended in this passage: ״And it shall come to pass, when all these things have come upon you, the blessing and the curse, which I have set before you, and you bring them back to your heart״ — that you should consider a matter in your heart. And what is that matter? ״Where HaShem your Elohim has driven you — הִדִּיחֲךָ — there.״ That is, you will think that HaShem your Elohim drove you away from the commandments with intention, essentially and in the first instance, because He desires neither you nor the observance of your commandments any longer; and through this you will despair of repentance, for you will say: if HaShem desired us, such things would not befall us from His hand, blessed be He. Concerning this it says: you think falsely about HaShem, and the matter is not so — rather, in your commandments He desires greatly, forever. And this matter is tested by what is said: ״and you shall return unto HaShem,״ etc. — for immediately, as soon as you resolve in your heart to return in complete repentance unto HaShem, even though you have not yet done the deed that is fit to be done in practice — for behold, it is not yet said here ״and you shall do all His commandments,״ as is said in the vicinity — by this He indicated with a finger to say: see how you err in your mind! For not only does He desire your commandments greatly, but even the moment you return unto HaShem and hearken to His voice according to all that I command you this day, with all your heart and with all your soul — for the essence of hearkening to the voice of HaShem is with all your heart alone, that HaShem may see in you signs of purity, that your heart is set toward HaShem — only that the obstacle is that you are unable to perform in practice all His commandments; nevertheless, immediately, ״then HaShem your Elohim will turn your captivity and have compassion on you, and He will return and gather you,״ etc." "Two times ״וְשָׁב״ (return/turn) are mentioned here, one after the other. The first speaks of the forgiveness of the preceding sin, which is called ״שְׁבוּתְךָ״ (your backsliding), as in ״Return, O backsliding — שׁוֹבֵבָה — daughter״ (Jeremiah 31:21), and as in ״and he walked backsliding — שׁוֹבֵב — in the way of his heart״ (Isaiah 57:17), and as in ״Return, O backsliding — מְשׁוּבָה — Israel״ (Jeremiah 3:12), and so there are many. Concerning this it says: ״then HaShem your Elohim will turn your backsliding — שְׁבוּתְךָ — and have compassion on you,״ for even ״your backsliding,״ which is attributed to you — that is, the sin — will be transformed into merit, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yoma 86b) that through repentance, deliberate sins are made as though merits. This is what is said: ״וְרִחֲמֶךָ — and have compassion on you,״ which is an expression of love — meaning that the Holy One, blessed be He, will love your backsliding, because the deliberate sins will be in His eyes, blessed be He, like merits, and He will love them greatly." "And the second speaks of the return of Israel from exile; this is what is said: ״and He will return and gather you from all the peoples where HaShem your Elohim has scattered you — הֱפִיצְךָ — there.״ Not as you thought, that HaShem your Elohim drove you — הִדִּיחֲךָ — and intended, essentially and in the first instance, to thrust you away from observance of the commandments; rather, essentially and in the first instance HaShem intended to scatter you. However, the thrusting away from the commandments followed as a by-product of the scattering. And in any case, ״if your driven one — נִדַּחֲךָ — be at the uttermost part of heaven״ — for in truth you are thrust away from the commandments, but one may not attribute the matter to Him, blessed be He, but rather to yourself, and it is called ״your driven one — נִדַּחֲךָ,״ for you did it and caused this thrusting away for yourself, from within and from without. And even though you caused yourself to be thrust away from the place of fulfillment of the commandments, which is very distant — nevertheless, ״from there HaShem your Elohim will gather you, and from there He will take you.״ ״From there He will gather you״ — this is the ingathering of the exiles; ״and from there He will take you״ — to Himself, to aid you in observance of the commandments. ״And He will bring you into the land, etc., and He will do you good and multiply you above your fathers.״ The meaning of ״above your fathers״ is not ״more than your fathers,״ for behold in the vicinity it says, ״as He rejoiced over your fathers,״ which implies not more than them but equal to them. Rather, ״from your fathers״ — to tell you: since you prepared your heart toward HaShem but your thought has not yet gone forth into action, for you have not yet fulfilled the commandments, therefore your merit is not yet sufficient for Him to do good to you and multiply you; rather, it is the merit of your fathers that stood for you to do good to you and multiply you. This is what is said: ״from your fathers״ — this matter is drawn to you from your fathers. However, in any case, since your heart is whole with HaShem, and you have resolved in your heart to do all the commandments of HaShem also in practice should a commandment come to your hand — correspondingly, HaShem will complete on your behalf." "<b>And HaShem your Elohim will circumcise your heart, and the heart of your seed, to love HaShem.</b> That He will place in your heart to serve Him out of love, not out of fear — for one who acts out of love serves HaShem with joy. But all the while that the Holy One, blessed be He, is not continuously taking vengeance on your enemies, the fear of your enemies is upon you, lest they return to wage war against you, and therefore your heart is not free to serve HaShem with joy. Therefore, ״and HaShem your Elohim will put all these curses upon your enemies, and upon those who hate you, who pursued you״ — and from then you will be secure that they will not come upon you anymore, and ״the righteous shall rejoice when he sees vengeance״ (Psalms 58:11) — behold, from then you will be ready to perform all the commandments of HaShem with joy, also in practice. This is what is said: ״and you shall return and hearken to the voice of HaShem, and do all His commandments״ — and then you will not need the merit of your fathers. This is what is said: ״and you — וְאַתָּה,״ for on you alone does the matter depend. And then HaShem will return to rejoice over you as He rejoiced over your fathers, for you will be equal to your fathers, since you have your own merit in your hand. And ״upon the mountains he did not eat״ — as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Sanhedrin 81a) from the verse ״upon the mountains he has not eaten״ (Ezekiel 18:6) — that he did not rely on the merit of his fathers. And through this explanation, many difficulties throughout the continuation of this passage are resolved, as will be clear to every understanding person with a little reflection."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:12.417799969Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}